Targeted unit tests for same-scheme non-default redirect ports and `Response.is_permanent_redirect`

## Summary

While reviewing redirect handling in `requests/sessions.py` and `requests/models.py`, two specific edge cases in the test suite stood out that would benefit from direct regression test coverage:

1. **Non-default port authentication stripping (`SessionRedirectMixin.should_strip_auth`)**:
   Under RFC 7235 / RFC 9110 §15.4 (Redirection), credentials must not leak across distinct origins. While `test_should_strip_auth_port_change` tests cross-scheme port changes (`http:1234` -> `https:4321`), there are no explicit unit tests verifying:
   - Same-scheme redirects between differing non-default ports (`http://example.com:8080/foo` -> `http://example.com:9090/bar` and `https://example.com:8443/foo` -> `https://example.com:9443/bar`) must strip auth.
   - Same-scheme redirects to the exact same non-default port (`http://example.com:8080/foo` -> `http://example.com:8080/bar`) must retain auth.

2. **RFC 7231 & RFC 7538 permanent redirect detection (`Response.is_permanent_redirect`)**:
   `Response.is_permanent_redirect` checks whether a response represents an HTTP permanent redirect (`301 Moved Permanently` or `308 Permanent Redirect` with a `Location` header present). While `Response.is_redirect` has end-to-end integration tests, `is_permanent_redirect` has no direct unit test asserting status code boundaries (`301`/`308` vs temporary `302`/`303`/`307`/`200`) or ensuring `False` is returned when `Location` is absent.

## Proposed Tests

The following additions to `tests/test_requests.py` establish explicit contracts for both behaviors:

```python
        # Differing non-default ports on the same scheme must strip auth
        assert s.should_strip_auth(
            "http://example.com:8080/foo", "http://example.com:9090/bar"
        )
        assert s.should_strip_auth(
            "https://example.com:8443/foo", "https://example.com:9443/bar"
        )
        # Identical non-default ports on the same scheme must retain auth
        assert not s.should_strip_auth(
            "http://example.com:8080/foo", "http://example.com:8080/bar"
        )
        assert not s.should_strip_auth(
            "https://example.com:8443/foo", "https://example.com:8443/bar"
        )
```

and parameterized boundary test:

```python
    @pytest.mark.parametrize(
        "status_code, has_location, expected",
        (
            (301, True, True),
            (308, True, True),
            (302, True, False),
            (303, True, False),
            (307, True, False),
            (200, True, False),
            (301, False, False),
            (308, False, False),
        ),
    )
    def test_is_permanent_redirect(self, status_code, has_location, expected):
        r = requests.Response()
        r.status_code = status_code
        if has_location:
            r.headers["location"] = "https://example.com/redirect"
        assert r.is_permanent_redirect is expected
```

All 16 test cases execute cleanly (0 failures) and are linted with `ruff`.

Pull requests seem to be restricted to repository collaborators on this repo, but the commit with tests and clean history is available on my fork here if useful:
